WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:02.100
Before we move on though, what I do want to cover,

00:02.100 --> 00:03.760
there was a question in the chat,

00:03.760 --> 00:05.020
which is actually a great one,

00:05.020 --> 00:06.580
regarding how do you actually generate

00:06.580 --> 00:08.620
unique keys on the front end.

00:08.620 --> 00:13.620
To be clear, so the thing that I've shown you over this workshop,

00:13.620 --> 00:17.100
let's see, if we take a look at here, okay.

00:17.100 --> 00:19.260
Line 32. As you can see,

00:19.260 --> 00:21.260
this is something that I've cobbled together myself, right?

00:21.260 --> 00:23.540
I'm using ES6 template literals to say,

00:23.540 --> 00:26.660
I'm going to guess at a unique data type which is user,

00:26.660 --> 00:28.540
and then I'm just going to attach the ID to it,

00:28.540 --> 00:31.780
even though I know the ID is not as unique.

00:31.780 --> 00:33.460
So this works, I think,

00:33.460 --> 00:34.980
when you're just prototyping and working on things,

00:34.980 --> 00:37.400
but obviously on an enterprise-grade application,

00:37.400 --> 00:38.740
that becomes a very different matter, right?

00:38.740 --> 00:42.040
Because you're all of a sudden contingent on

00:42.040 --> 00:44.660
the fact that someone made the right choice to scope the name correctly,

00:44.660 --> 00:48.660
and that's not something we ever want to rely on in terms of reducing human error.

00:48.660 --> 00:51.380
So when it comes to actually generating unique IDs,

00:51.380 --> 00:54.380
granted, ideally, your backend is doing that for you,

00:54.380 --> 00:57.420
but in the event you're not actually getting that information,

00:57.420 --> 01:03.180
the NPM module I really highly recommend is the UUID module.

01:03.180 --> 01:07.740
So basically, how it works is you'll install UUID on your project,

01:07.740 --> 01:09.700
and you basically, as you can see here,

01:09.700 --> 01:12.660
you import it at the top of your file,

01:12.660 --> 01:15.180
and then as part of creating the new form.

01:15.180 --> 01:19.260
So actually, this does happen a lot if you're creating on the front-end side especially,

01:19.260 --> 01:21.700
creating new data that you might be sending to the backend.

01:21.700 --> 01:25.100
So you might actually have a case where I'm going to just pseudo-code this,

01:25.100 --> 01:28.780
we're not going to install it because that would just add to the bloat.

01:28.780 --> 01:30.580
But for the sake of demoing this,

01:30.580 --> 01:33.180
let me just go ahead and at the top here.

01:33.180 --> 01:35.580
So you can imagine that what we would do is we would say,

01:35.580 --> 01:36.860
we would copy this line,

01:36.860 --> 01:40.900
say, okay, we're going to use the UUID library.

01:40.900 --> 01:48.180
Then again, assuming we had a new method called add new user,

01:48.180 --> 01:52.260
you could then say this.userList.push,

01:52.260 --> 01:53.660
and when you create that new object,

01:53.740 --> 01:54.620
this is where you're going to go,

01:54.620 --> 02:02.860
the ID is going to be UUID v4 run like this.

02:02.860 --> 02:06.220
Then typically, you probably would have some new user object that you have in

02:06.220 --> 02:09.820
your reactive data that contains like name, again, let's say Ben.

02:09.820 --> 02:14.900
So what I typically do is I just spread out everything from this.newUser,

02:14.900 --> 02:19.380
and then I basically override in case there's any IDs that are attached to it,

02:19.380 --> 02:21.740
this ensures that it's being generated on the fly,

02:21.740 --> 02:25.820
and then that's basically how I would approach generating a unique ID on the front end.

